.TH SYSJAR 1 "November 2026" "OpenMandriva" "System jars"
.SH NAME
sysjar \- build Maven, Gradle, Ant and Ivy projects against system jars
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysjar
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ --
.IR "tool arguments" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysjar
treats jars installed by the distribution as shared libraries. A project
that asks Maven or Gradle for
.I org.slf4j:slf4j-api:1.7.25
is built against the one
.I slf4j-api
jar the distribution shipped, whatever version that jar is. Nothing is
downloaded. The build fails with the name of each missing library instead
of reaching the network.
.PP
Installed libraries are read from
.I /usr/share/sysjar/index/*/*.sysjar
and, for packages built before sysjar, from XMvn fragments in
.IR /usr/share/maven-metadata .
Jars stay in
.I /usr/share/java
and POMs in
.IR /usr/share/maven-poms .
One coordinate has one version. A newer
.I .sysjar
record replaces a legacy fragment of the same coordinate.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Print groupId:artifactId, the system version, and the file.
.TP
.BI resolve " COORD"
Print the jar or POM for
.I COORD
and ignore the version the caller asked for.
.I COORD
is
.IR group:artifact[:version][:classifier][@type] .
.TP
.BI classpath " COORD..."
Print a colon-separated classpath.
.TP
.B repo --dest DIR
Write a Maven repository of symlinks to system jars. POMs in that
repository have their dependency versions rewritten to system versions.
The default destination is
.IR /var/cache/sysjar/m2 .
.TP
.B rewrite PATH
Rewrite
.I pom.xml
and
.I ivy.xml
files under
.I PATH
so known libraries use the system version. Reactor modules are left
alone.
.B --drop
.I group:artifact
removes that dependency or plugin.
.TP
.B mvn -- \fIMVN-ARGS\fR
Rewrite the project, write a private repository and settings, and run
Maven against only that repository.
.B --drop
.I group:artifact
removes that dependency or plugin from the project and from parent POMs
published into the private repository, so a plugin that arrives only
through a parent profile is removed there too. Maven is not passed
.BR -o :
that switch ignores a file repository as well as the network. The
settings mirror every repository at the private one, so a missing jar
fails the build instead of being downloaded. A missing compile or runtime dependency
stops the command before Maven starts.
.PP
Maven's default lifecycle plugins are not packages. When
.I /usr/share/maven/toolchain
exists, files there are linked into the private repository wherever
the index did not already publish a file. A later link does not replace
an index file. Those jars are not indexed, so a project dependency is
still missing when the distribution has not packaged it. Parent POMs
that no package provides are read from
.IR /usr/share/sysjar/parents ,
the pom-only coordinates from javapackages-bootstrap. Bootstrap jars
are not loaded.
A plugin pin is moved to the toolchain's version when that plugin is
not an installed package, because Maven resolves the pin before the
version built into maven-core. A POM copied into the private
repository gets the same pin, so a version inherited from a parent
resolves to the toolchain jar.
.PP
A plugin or build extension that is not installed is removed from the
project being built, except a plugin that produces the main artifact
(compiler, resources, jar, war, ear, assembly, shade, antrun, bundle,
build-helper, moditect, and the plugin plugin). A pluginManagement
entry that only pins a version stays, unless the toolchain has that
plugin, in which case the pin is moved to the toolchain version. One
that names no version is removed when the plugin is not installed,
because Maven resolves that version before it runs the build. One that declares executions is
removed when the plugin is not installed, because Maven resolves it
while loading the project. A reporting plugin that is not installed is
removed too. The removed plugin is reported. It does not run.
An import-scoped BOM that is not installed is removed. Maven resolves
it while building the model. Other dependencyManagement entries stay.
A test-scoped dependency that is not installed is removed for the same
reason: Maven resolves it even when tests are skipped. An installed
test dependency stays. A provided dependency stays, because the
compiler can import it.
A dependency POM copied into the private repository loses those same
uninstalled plugins.
.B --drop
still removes a plugin that this leaves in place, including one that
produces the main artifact. Maven's built-in lifecycle plugins are pointed at the
installed jar. Compiler source, target and release values below 8 are
raised to 8, including in those published POMs, because current JDKs
reject the older levels. Raw
.B -source
and
.B -target
entries in a compiler plugin's
.B compilerArguments
are removed. javac rejects them together with
.BR --release ,
and the plugin already passes the language level.
.B com.sun:tools
and
.B jdk.tools:jdk.tools
are removed: they are the JDK, not a jar. A version written as exactly
SNAPSHOT becomes 0-SNAPSHOT.
.B --allow-missing
continues anyway. Test-scoped gaps are warnings.
.B --mvn
.I PATH
selects the Maven launcher. The default is
.B mvn
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B gradle -- \fIGRADLE-ARGS\fR
Run Gradle
.B --offline
with an init script that forces system versions and a private Gradle
user home. The init script also writes
.I .sysjar/reactor/*.json
when the
.B java
plugin's
.B jar
task runs, which
.B install --auto
reads afterwards.
.B --keep-repos
leaves repositories declared by the build in place.
.B --user-home
uses the default Gradle home.
.B --gradle
.I PATH
selects the Gradle launcher.
.TP
.B ant -- \fIANT-ARGS\fR
Rewrite
.I ivy.xml
files, write
.I ivysettings.xml
for the system repository, and run Ant. Ant is given
.B -Divy.settings.file
when the tree has an Ivy module. A project that has no
.I ivysettings.xml
gets one beside each
.IR ivy.xml ,
which is the file a plain
.B ivy:resolve
loads. An existing
.I ivysettings.xml
is left in place. A
.I build.xml
that names its own settings file ignores
.BR -Divy.settings.file .
.B --ant
.I PATH
selects the Ant launcher. The default target is the one in
.IR build.xml .
A later
.B -f
overrides the build file sysjar selected.
.TP
.B ivy
Rewrite Ivy revisions and write
.I ivysettings.xml
for the system repository.
.TP
.B install --buildroot DIR
Copy a jar and POM into the buildroot and write the
.I .sysjar
record RPM uses for dependencies.
.B --auto
discovers reactor POMs, Gradle records, or an Ivy module.
.B --artifact
.I POM[:JAR]
selects one artifact.
.B --alias
.I group:artifact
adds another name for that same jar.
.B --file-name
sets the file name under
.IR /usr/share/java .
.B --link
.I NAME
adds a symlink of that name, without
.IR .jar .
It is a file name, not another Maven coordinate.
.B --filelist
.I FILE
writes the rpm
.B %files
list: the jar, its symlinks, the POM, and the index file.
.B %sysjar_install
passes
.BR --filelist\ .mfiles .
.TP
.B scan PATH
Show each dependency and whether a system jar provides it. Gradle
files are scanned as text, so those lines are marked heuristic.
.TP
.B builddep PATH
Print one dependency name per line, for
.BR %generate_buildrequires .
A
.B BuildRequires:
prefix is not printed: rpm would record that word as its own dependency.
Exits 1 when something is not installed, and still prints that name.
.B --allow-missing
omits names that are not installed and exits 0.
.TP
.B check
Report index entries whose jar or POM is not on disk.
.TP
.BR provides ", " requires
Print RPM dependency lines for one
.I .sysjar
file. The same scripts are wired up as an RPM file attribute.
.SH PACKAGING
.nf
BuildRequires:  sysjar
BuildRequires:  mvn(org.slf4j:slf4j-api)

%build
%sysjar_mvn -DskipTests package

%install
%sysjar_install
.fi
.PP
The same steps are available as declarative build systems. Installing
this package provides
.BR rpm-build(maven) ,
.BR rpm-build(gradle) ,
.BR rpm-build(ant)
and
.BR rpm-build(ivy) ,
which is what
.B BuildSystem:
requires. A spec can be:
.PP
.nf
BuildSystem: maven
BuildOption: -DskipTests
.fi
.PP
.B %build
runs
.B sysjar mvn -Dmaven.test.skip=true package
plus the
.B BuildOption
and
.B BuildOption(build)
words. A word that does not start with a dash is a goal and replaces
.BR package ,
so
.B BuildOption(build): compile
does not also run
.BR package .
.B %install
runs
.BR "sysjar install --auto" .
.B BuildOption(install): --file-name demo
is passed through.
.B BuildOption(install): --artifact pom:jar
installs that pair instead of discovering one.
.B BuildSystem: gradle
defaults to
.BR assemble .
.B BuildSystem: ant
runs Ant's default target, and
.B BuildSystem: ivy
is that same Ant build. The static build requirement is the launcher
package
.RB ( maven ,
.B gradle
or
.BR ant ).
.B %check
runs
.B test
when the spec defines
.BR "%bcond_without tests" .
.B %generate_buildrequires
prints
.B mvn()
lines for the unpacked tree, including libraries that are not installed yet.
A package that is itself the bootstrap, such as Maven, sets
.B %global sysjar_bootstrap 1
and
.B %global _sysjar_mvn
to the stage-0 launcher. The static build requirement is then
.B javapackages-bootstrap
instead of
.BR maven ,
and sysjar is passed
.BR --bootstrap .
.B %global sysjar_bootstrap_root
points at an extracted tree.
.B %global sysjar_drops
is passed through, usually as repeated
.B --drop
coordinates.
.B %global sysjar_extra
is passed through in front of those drops. A second index root goes here.
.B --root
replaces the default
.IR / ,
so keep the system index by repeating it:
.B --root / --root /tmp/resolver-root .
Leave
.B sysjar_extra
unset in the spec when a command-line
.B \-\-define
should supply the root. A spec
.B %global
of the same name would hide that define.
.B %global _sysjar_java_version
pins one JDK for a maven, gradle, ant or ivy build.
Unset, the build uses the latest:
.B java-current-devel
at build time,
.B %{_sysjar_java_requires}
is
.BR java-current ,
and the section keeps
.BR $JAVA_HOME .
.B %global _sysjar_java_version 21
selects
.B java-21-openjdk-devel
and
.BR java-21-openjdk ,
and exports
.B JAVA_HOME
as
.IR %{_jvmdir}/java-21-openjdk .
Use that only when a tool cannot read the current class-file major.
.B %global _sysjar_java_home
overrides the path and does not change those package names.
.PP
The installed record provides
.B mvn(groupId:artifactId)
at the project version, which is the name existing cooker packages
already require. Classifiers use the same shape as those packages,
for example
.BR mvn(commons-logging:commons-logging::api:) .
Requires are generated for direct compile and runtime dependencies.
Optional, test, provided and system-scoped dependencies are not
required at install time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --root " DIR"
Read the index from
.I DIR
instead of
.IR / .
Repeat to overlay one root on another. A
.I .sysjar
record wins over an XMvn fragment. At the same priority the later,
or the higher, version wins.
.TP
.BI --bootstrap-root " DIR"
Also read
.I DIR/usr/share/javapackages-bootstrap/maven-metadata
as a fallback. Those coordinates lose to any real package. Use this to
compile a package before its dependencies are packaged on their own.
.B --bootstrap
reads that metadata from each
.BR --root .
A bootstrap coordinate can have more than one version. The build keeps
the one it asked for. A dependency that leaves the version to a parent
keeps doing that, so the managed version is the one that is matched. A
real package replaces every bootstrap version.
.TP
.B -q
Suppress the rewrite log and warnings. Errors are still printed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/java
Shared jars.
.TP
.I /usr/share/maven-poms
Upstream POMs. sysjar rewrites a copy when it builds a repository. It
does not edit the installed POM.
.TP
.I /usr/share/sysjar/index
One JSON record per artifact.
.TP
.I /usr/share/maven-metadata
Legacy XMvn fragments. Still read.
.TP
.I /var/cache/sysjar/m2
Maven repository rebuilt by a file trigger when the index changes.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR build-classpath (1)
.SH CAVEATS
Gradle coordinates written only in a version catalog alias, or computed
in a plugin, are not visible to
.B scan
until Gradle has resolved them. A multi-module Maven build resolves its
own modules itself.
.B sysjar
does not replace those modules with whatever older jar happens to be
installed.
.PP
Parent POMs have to be installed too. Maven will not build an effective
model without them. A POM file that is not XML is replaced, in the
repository only, by one generated from the index dependency list. The
installed file is left alone. A jar that contains
.I META-INF/maven/plugin.xml
is published as a Maven plugin. Dependencies that the installed
.B maven-core
jar exports
.RI ( Meta-INF/maven/extension.xml )
are omitted from every generated POM except
.BR maven-core 's
own. Those jars are already on Maven's runtime classpath.
